# House rules for agents on the resident lane

One model serves this session and every other client on the pair. Each step
is one request to it, and the whole transcript is sent again on every step.

## Never talk to the model stack from the shell

- Do not call ports 30000, 20127 or 20128 on 127.0.0.1 or localhost with
  curl, wget, nc, python or anything else. They are the engine, the clamp and
  the router that are answering you right now. A shell call to them queues
  behind your own turn and tells you nothing.
- Do not test, benchmark, warm up or health-check the model.
- Do not start, stop or restart a lane, a container or a gateway unless the
  user asked for exactly that.

## Never sleep-poll

- Do not run `sleep`, alone, in a loop, or wrapped (`timeout 30 sleep 5`,
  `bash -c "sleep 5"`, `python -c "time.sleep(5)"`, `read -t 5`).
- To wait for a command, run it in the foreground and set the bash tool's
  `timeout` to cover it. The call returns when the command exits.
- To wait for a state, use one blocking command with its own bound:
  `docker wait NAME`, `timeout 600 tail --pid=PID -f /dev/null`,
  `curl --retry 10 --retry-connrefused --max-time 60 URL` (never the three
  ports above).
- If a job needs longer than one call can cover, start it detached with its
  output in a log file, tell the user what is running and how to check it,
  and stop. Do not come back to poll it.

## Keep each step small

- Search before reading. Read ranges, not whole files.
- Cap command output: `| tail -n 40`, quiet flags, a grep for the line you
  need.
- Put independent read-only checks in one command or one message.
- Do not re-run a command that already succeeded. Do not re-read a file you
  just wrote.

## Step budget

- Build has 60 steps per user message. Plan the change, make it, verify once.
- When the limit arrives, say what is done and what is left. The user
  continues with one message.
